    "q-131930": {
      "dom_id": "step-q131930",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$, where\n\n$$\nr(s)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\,1,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡s⟩,\\,0\\le s<2\\pi .\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡s,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡s+{cos}^{2}⁡s} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
